WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:08.040
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again. Like everyone
else, sometimes I just need to laugh the pain

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.440
away. Sometimes the crushing anxiety of existence
may feel like too much; but, hey, that’s

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:20.310
why I have a dog. It helps. We at Wisecrack
love us some M.P. These wily Brits redefined

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:25.149
comedy in the 1970s -- and are still hugely
influential today. Just look at comedies today

00:00:25.149 --> 00:00:30.660
- South Park, Rick and Morty or even Deadpool.
All hilarious in their own unique ways; but,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.520
without Monty Python, they probably wouldn’t
even exist. Monty Python paved the way for

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.600
comedy as we know it, combining absurdity,
satire and postmodern-reflexivity into some

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:48.510
of the most influential and quoted comedies
ever. So because there’s nothing funnier

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:52.490
than explaining a joke to death -- Welcome
to a Wisecrack Edition on the Legacy of Monty

00:00:52.490 --> 00:01:04.400
Python. Like a hipster in a gluten-free juice
bar, postmodernism is everywhere these days.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.921
Jurassic World, Star Wars, Family Guy, pretty
much anything that you can think of. These

00:01:08.921 --> 00:01:13.689
films and TV shows constantly refer back on
themselves, commenting on the narrative conventions

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:16.979
and iconography of their predecessors... "It's
another Death Star."

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:25.880
"I wish that were the case, Major. This was
the Death Star. This is Starkiller Base."

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.670
...Or just copying them wholesale.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:33.159
But nothing encapsulates this movement more
than Deadpool. Heck -- we even did an entire

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.189
‘Philosophy Of’ on this exact subject.
Everyone loves Deadpool because, quite simply,

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:41.579
he doesn’t give a f***. He’s rebelliously
self-aware, repeatedly winking at his own

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:49.819
narrative and mocking the superhero genre.
"Okay, let's pro/con this Superhero thing..."

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:54.579
This constant self-reflexivity encapsulates
Postmodernism, first coined by the French

00:01:54.579 --> 00:02:01.119
philosopher Jean-Francois Lyotard in 1979.
For Lyotard -- all stories previously fit

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:07.170
into oft-told grand mythologies. Great heroes
on a voyage for some grandiose goal. Or as

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.670
he referred to them -- metanarratives. Per
Lyotard - these metanarratives were stories

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:16.810
that provided a pattern and structure for
people’s beliefs, teaching viewers a universal

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:21.569
insight. Think of The Wizard of Oz -- and
how, stripped of the flying monkeys and melting

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:28.130
witches, it’s actually just a story about
a girl realizing, well…

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:32.430
Or even The Fast and The Furious -- underneath
all the ludicrous car stunts, there’s a

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.599
deeper meta-narrative about the importance
of family. Through these stories -- morals

00:02:36.599 --> 00:02:42.290
&amp; social customs are taught and reinforced.
But postmodernism subverts these grand narratives,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:46.959
revealing the chaos and disorder beneath.
Like, say, Deadpool, a comic-book character

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:58.930
who constantly tears down everything that
comic books stand for. But as edgy as Deadpool

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:04.069
may seem, he AIN’T GOT S*** on the Pythons
-- who took postmodern deconstruction to a

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:10.329
whole other level. Case in point: their all-time
classic -- Monty Python and the Holy Grail.

00:03:10.329 --> 00:03:15.280
Whereas Deadpool lampoons only the comic book
metanarrative, Monty Python and the Holy Grail

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:21.640
is DEFINED by breaking down multiple metanarratives.
Among them: Arthurian chivalry, Christianity,

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:27.469
logic and the very film medium itself. The
first of these metanarratives, Arthurian Chivalry,

00:03:27.469 --> 00:03:32.799
first evolved in the Late Middle Ages, idealizing
knights as honourable and self sacrificing.

00:03:32.799 --> 00:03:38.110
You know -- the usual knightly s*** like protecting
the weak, showing mercy to enemies, never

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:43.579
lying and championing justice and truth. But
Monty Python and the Holy Grail completely

00:03:43.579 --> 00:03:49.280
dismantles and mocks these time-tested tenets.
"Who's that, then?" "Must be a king." "Why?"

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.109
"'cuz he hasn't got s*** all over him."

00:03:53.109 --> 00:03:58.049
When Sir Robin encounters a three-headed giant,
his minstrels sing the heroic narrative everyone

00:03:58.049 --> 00:04:10.310
expects — 
but Robin himself runs the hell away as quickly

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:18.049
as possible. "Brave sir Robin Ran Away." "No!"
"Bravely ran away away..." and so on. Similarly

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:22.620
-- in the Arthurian Legend, Arthur’s arrival
at Camelot is usually treated as an emotional

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:30.930
high-point in his story… Yet Monty Python
undercuts the seriousness of the moment, turning

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:46.190
this hallmark event into a goofy musical number.
And then to top it off -- Arthur decides not

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:53.740
to even bother going to Camelot. Each of our
supposed ‘great heroes’ are revealed to

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:58.990
be far less than they claim. The power-hungry
Arthur demands that every peasant acknowledge

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:08.509
him as their king; but if they dare question
his rule, he immediately attacks them. "What

00:05:08.509 --> 00:05:17.460
I object to is you automatically treating
me like an inferior." "Well, I *am* King..."

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:22.120
Sir Galahad the Pure, the most ‘faithful’
of knights, is seduced in less than four minutes

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.960
by a “company of a hundred-and-sixty lonely
women”. And when Lancelot receives a note

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:32.110
from a distressed maiden, he rushes to rescue
her; but in the process, murders dozens of

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:35.900
unarmed people. Then, in the end, it turns
out -- there wasn’t even a maiden to save

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:43.500
in the first place. Monty Python uses postmodernism
to illustrate how the stories people tell

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.350
themselves enablesome awful s***; Lancelot
and Arthur’s own metanarratives justifying

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:52.150
their violent and oppressive acts. The fact
that these metanarratives aren’t even true

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:56.050
to begin with is just the icing on the cake
- as our knightly heroes are revealed to be

00:05:56.050 --> 00:06:04.099
cowards, hypocrites, and violent sociopaths.
"I just get carried away..."

00:06:04.099 --> 00:06:09.440
Even God is put into doubt. Per the Christianity
metanarrative, God is good and all powerful

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.780
and has a plan for humanity. Yet in Monty
Python and the Holy Grail, God isn't great

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:20.021
and kind at all. He's just a cranky dick,
who’s really crappily animated. Philosopher

00:06:20.021 --> 00:06:25.790
Mikhail Bakhtin defined this as the ‘downward-swing’
- which I swear isn’t a baseball term. Per

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:30.669
Bakhtin -- most stories are upward swing(s)
-- in that they “elevate a story to a symbolic

00:06:30.669 --> 00:06:35.300
state, [and make] them universal.” On the
contrary, in a downward swing - the classical

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:39.930
story becomes so distorted and grotesque that
you don’t even know whether to scream, cry

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:45.139
or laugh. And it’s not just the story that
becomes distorted; but also we, the readers

00:06:45.139 --> 00:06:50.360
and viewers, ourselves. Every lesson we learned
through the metanarrative is now cast into

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.630
doubt right alongside it. Take one of the
most famous scenes in The Holy Grail -- the

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:59.949
witch trial. Sir Bedevere the Wise puts a
poor woman through a ‘foolproof’ logic

00:06:59.949 --> 00:07:04.220
test in order to determine if she’s a witch.
Per Bedevere - witches burn at the stake just

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:08.750
like wood and wood floats on water just like
a duck. So if the woman is a witch -- she

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:13.150
will weigh the same as a duck. Monty Python
here takes formal logic and distorts it into

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:18.630
a grotesque version of itself, in effect casting
doubt our own fundamental faith in logic.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:23.229
If classical logic can be distorted into justifying
a woman’s death, then how can it be truly

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:27.919
trusted at all? By the end of The Holy Grail
-- this distortion extends to the very film

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:32.370
itself. As Arthur prepares for an epic battle
with the French at the Castle Aaargh, modern

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:36.039
day police suddenly arrive and arrest the
king for murder. The police then push and

00:07:36.039 --> 00:07:39.909
cover the camera lens, breaking the fourth
wall -- making the audience cognizant of the

00:07:39.909 --> 00:07:44.840
fact they’re watching a movie. The Holy
Grail constantly pokes fun at itself as a

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:48.710
‘movie’. For instance - the film’s title
credits are undermined when Swedish subtitles

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:53.490
suddenly appear, promoting the country as
a terrific vacation spot. The credit-makers

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:58.430
are then promptly fired, causing the credit
format to completely change. Throughout the

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:03.680
film, Arthur uses coconuts instead of a horse
due to the film’s tight budget. Arthur even

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.470
refers to one character as -- "it's the old
man from scene 24."

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:11.909
And just to hammer the point home -- the knights
defeat the Legendary Black Beast of Arghhh,

00:08:11.909 --> 00:08:16.500
not through any battle or test of wills; but
because the animator that designed the creature

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:21.460
has a heart-attack. This self-reflexivity
reveals The Holy Grail to be its own metanarrative

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:26.650
- film just as artificial as the chivalrous
Arthurian heroes within. "Do you think this

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:30.570
scene should have been cut? We were so worried
while the boys were writing it... but now

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:36.180
we're glad! It's better than some of the previous
scenes, I think!" That’s just how postmodern

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:41.560
Monty Python and The Holy Grail truly is - it
deconstructs religion &amp; logic within a deconstruction

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:50.510
of heroic tropes within a deconstruction of
the film medium itself. Basically -- the ‘Inception’

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:54.640
of postmodern film. There’s probably no
show as popular on the inter-webs as Rick

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.880
and Morty. I mean -- Wisecrack’s really
just a domain switch away from becoming a

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:03.529
Rick and Morty Fan Club. And that’s because
the show always ties its absurd humor with

00:09:03.529 --> 00:09:08.510
thought-provoking insight -- like, well, on
the meaninglessness of life. "Yes! I did it!

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:13.110
There is no God! In your face!" Take the ‘Get
Schwifty’ episode. When massive heads suddenly

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:17.540
appear all across the planet, many people
begin to worship the heads as Gods, starting

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:23.050
up a cult and even sacrificing people to appease
them. Of course, in reality, these heads aren’t

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:27.600
actually Gods at all; but an alien race that
just wants Earth to participate in an American

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.640
Idol-like reality show.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:34.610
This type of humor is known as “The Comedy
of the Absurd”. This existential-based comedy

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:38.840
focused on the fallout when confronted with
a world devoid of meaning. However instead

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:44.130
of wallowing in some murky woe-is-me despair,
the absurd takes the opposite approach - pointing

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:50.240
out the broad humor in our own tragic insignificance.
This tragic absurdity is foundational to Monty

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:54.640
Python’s brand of comedy. In their third
feature, The Life of Brian, poor Brian’s

00:09:54.640 --> 00:10:00.960
entire existence is one big absurd misunderstanding.
The humble peasant is born in the stable next

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.010
to Jesus, confusing the Three Wise Men - who
assume the not-special-at-all Brian to be

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:16.940
the Messiah. The projection of significance
on the insignificant is a hallmark of existential

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:22.170
thought -- in particular our boy Albert Camus,
whod rejected this desperate search for meaning,and

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:27.510
instead embraced, the, you guessed it, “The
Absurd”. When Brian runs away from his disciples,

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:32.460
they mistakenly believe he’s risen to heaven.
And later when Brian loses a sandal, these

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:34.600
same disciples proclaim the sandal his sign.
"The shoe is a sign! Let us follow his example!"

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:35.600
"What?" "Let us like him hold up one shoe
and let the other be upon our foot for this

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:36.600
is his sign that all who follow him shall
do likewise!"

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.990
Of course -- Brian didn’t mean for the sandal
to be anything more than footwear; and yet

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:50.200
people -- in their desire for meaning -- attribute
significance to even the silliest of objects.

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:54.870
We see this exact same misappropriation of
meaning in Get Schwifty as Principal Vagina

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:59.310
and the rest of his cult mistake the Alien
Heads as Gods, when in reality they’re only

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:04.640
Reality TV show junkies. Comedies of the absurd
draw their humor from this discrepancy, between

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:09.690
what is and what isn’t, between what’s
true and what people believe to be true. Characters

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:14.430
in these absurd comedies are often are caught
in hopeless situations, forced into repetitive

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:19.269
and ultimately fruitless actions. Like Brian
who constantly tries to tell his disciples

00:11:19.269 --> 00:11:25.931
he’s not the Messiah to little avail. “Only
the true Messiah denies his divinity.” No

00:11:25.931 --> 00:11:31.231
matter what Brian does, his disciples grow
in number and become ever more fervent, all

00:11:31.231 --> 00:11:37.670
leading to a horrific and tragic conclusion:
crucifixion. Yet despite his impending doom,

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:45.200
Brian’s comforted by his fellow crucifixion
buddies, who break out into song -- The Bright

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:49.910
Side of Life.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:54.930
The crucifixion buddies cheerful lament reflects
the Myth of Sisyphus -- in which poor Sisyphus

00:11:54.930 --> 00:12:00.470
is cursed to roll a rock up a mountain only
for it to fall back down over and over again.

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:04.670
Existentialist Albert Camus famously applied
the greek myth to life itself, The trick though

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:08.899
is not to let this meaninglessness ever get
you down -- instead, in the words of Life

00:12:08.899 --> 00:12:13.160
of Brian, ‘Always look at the bright-side.’
Because when faced with our own insignificance

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:19.150
and impending doom, there’s only one real
option, though it may seem completely f***ing

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:26.290
absurd, laughter. Nowadays satire has become
more vital than ever whether via late night

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:30.540
talk shows like Full Frontal with Samantha
Bee or more traditional half hour comedies

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:35.720
like South Park. Hell the last three seasons
of South Park focus heavily on the 2016 election

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:42.899
and the Trump presidency. Political satires,
like South Park, derive their humor by revealing

00:12:42.899 --> 00:12:48.820
the hypocrisy in established rules and orders
-- a la the 2016 election and the PC brigade.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:53.649
But way before South Park, Monty Python paved
the way, subverting the established political

00:12:53.649 --> 00:12:57.910
ideologies of England. In fact - South Park’s
creators Trey Parker and Matt Stone often

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:03.790
cite Monty Python as the single biggest influence
on their show. "Matt &amp; I got along so well

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:06.100
because we're both huge Python freaks."

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:11.460
In Monty Python’s final and darkest feature,
The Meaning of Life, the Pythons openly comment

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:16.290
on English imperialism, economic disparity,
and the classicist social hierarchies of the

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:21.290
country. The opening mini-movie, The Crimson
Permanent Assurance, depicts a group of poor

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:26.190
elderly workers forced into a pirate-like
frenzy by young American yuppies. The old

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:30.529
workers rebel against these yuppie dicks,
throwing them out windows to their death -- and

00:13:30.529 --> 00:13:34.720
then proceed to destroy every shiny bright
corporate building in their path, bringing

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:38.970
down the capitalist system that literally
anchored them to the ground. The Pythons carry

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:43.009
this satirical look at capitalism throughout
the film, questioning the economic notion

00:13:43.009 --> 00:13:47.589
that self-interest results in the greater
good. In fact -- we see quite the opposite.

00:13:47.589 --> 00:13:51.050
In the sketch, the ‘Miracle of Birth’
-- the doctors are far more concerned with

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:55.100
their fancy equipment and impressing their
administrator than the poor woman they’re

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:58.780
operating on. "And get the most expensive
machines, in case the administrator comes."

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:03.230
Later -- in the sketch “Live Organ Transplants”,
two bureaucrats forcefully remove the liver

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:07.360
from an impoverished man for the good of the
country, remarking nonsensically that they

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:16.790
must kill the guy so that they can then take
his liver. Constantly we see people drowning

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:21.380
under this system. Britain’s Yorkshire is
ironically referred to as a “Third World

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:25.730
Country” -- and in the same sketch, a poor
man is forced to sell off his children to

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:31.261
science in order to make ends meet. This economic
disparity climaxes in the sketch “The Autumn

00:14:31.261 --> 00:14:36.360
Years”, where the grotesquely obese and
rich Mr. Creosote dines at a fancy restaurant,

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:43.709
eating more and more until he literally explodes.
The implications couldn’t be clearer -- the

00:14:43.709 --> 00:14:49.020
rich get richer and fatter at the expense
of the starving lower class. But it’s not

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:53.890
just social injustice that the Pythons skewer
- but the whole notion of British imperialism

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:58.600
in general. The hand of God strikes down a
military officer who insists Britain will

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:03.470
always need an army to put down differing
ideologies. And in the same sketch, a Sergeant

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:07.570
Major drills his platoon on whether they have
anything better to do than “march up and

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:22.431
down the town square.” Well -- it turns
out they do, like read a book or go to the

00:15:22.431 --> 00:15:26.870
movies or really anything at all. The Pythons
even state this central thesis at the end

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:30.139
of the picture… "Try and be nice to people,
avoid getting fat, read a good book every

00:15:30.139 --> 00:15:33.680
now and then, get some walking in, and try
to live together in peace and harmony with

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:35.190
people of all creeds and nations."

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:39.630
For The Pythons -- British imperialism,is
a threat to the meaning of life. This manifests

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:43.880
in their depictions of war - where soldiers
attempting to celebrate a birthday are gunned

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:49.899
down one by one - the brutal reality of war,
of supposed imperial conquest, juxtaposed

00:15:49.899 --> 00:15:54.120
against the simple pleasure of enjoying a
birthday cake. These ideas extend to their

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:58.850
sketch show The Flying Circus. The show oft
portrays bureaucrats as buffoons… or as

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:03.240
sticks-in-the-mud, constantly censoring the
show itself. "I'm not prepared to pursue my

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:08.029
line of inquiry any further as I think this
is getting too silly!" "Quite agree, quite

00:16:08.029 --> 00:16:14.490
agree. Silly silly silly. Right. Get on with
it. GET ON WITH IT!" As Christopher Hitchens

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:19.230
once said -- “the essential founding gag
of [Monty Python] is the bubbling magma of

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:24.710
absurdity that lay beneath the fragile crust
of British reserve. At any moment, a man with

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:29.720
a bowler hat or an umbrella might become a
raging cross-dresser or barking sadist.”

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:34.500
It’s this unpredictability, this mixture
between the postmodern, the political and

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:39.220
the absurd that makes Monty Python unlike
any comedy troupe that came before or after.

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:40.220
"Nobody expects the Spanish Inquisition!"

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:44.480
By merging together social commentary, existential
angst and just plain ol’ silliness - The

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:49.910
Pythons always kept you on your toes. They
defied convention, concocting a brand of comedy

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:55.149
so singular that a word had to be created
just for them -- Pythonesque. Today their

